{"comparisons":[],"failures":[],"frontier":[],"initial":{"normative":{"coverage":[{"reason":"Interpreted through competing responsibility readings: continuing allocation of responsibility and substantive performance.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"reason":"Interpreted as preserving full responsibility despite outsourcing; the reference to applicable Appendix factors remains an unresolved dependency.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"reason":"Interpreted as an express requirement concerning proper ownership records and operational compatibility, relevant to substantive fulfillment.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"reason":"Interpreted through competing readings of the request condition and its relationship to footnote 5.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"reason":"Interpreted as specifying minimum assurance content, with uncertainty about whether it independently requires demonstration and about confidence-standard attachment.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"reason":"Interpreted as request-conditioned procurement of third-party audit or verification, distinct from the provider's own confirmation and demonstration.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"reason":"Interpreted as request-conditioned procurement of a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"},{"reason":"Interpreted with the fuller duplicate in unit 8: register maintenance may be assigned to specified actors without displacing the Product Provider's retained responsibility.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802"},{"reason":"Interpreted as a distinct mandatory register-maintenance rule and request-conditioned disclosure of register location; its actors are not automatically identical to the Product Provider.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"reason":"Contextual comparison supplies an express valuation-specific selection and supervision standard; it cannot automatically supply the missing tokenisation standard.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"reason":"Contextual parallel preserves issuer-intermediary responsibility despite outsourcing, subject to its own issuance and intended dealing or advisory conditions.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"},{"reason":"Contextual intermediary duties concern due diligence and satisfaction with controls before related activities; they do not establish Product Provider fulfillment.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"dimensions":[{"dimension":"scope","explanation":"The central provisions address Product Providers. Register custodians, valuation management companies, issuer-intermediaries and dealing, advising or portfolio-managing intermediaries have separately stated roles and triggers. Role overlap requires facts; it cannot be assumed.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"definitions","explanation":"Ultimate or full responsibility, management and operational soundness, proper records, integrity, satisfactory legal opinion and high confidence lack operational definitions here. Retained accountability, supervisory conduct and achieved outcomes are distinguishable, but the text supplies no complete sufficiency test connecting them.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"exceptions","explanation":"Outsourcing is expressly denied exculpatory effect. The applicability qualification concerns the Appendix factors, not continued responsibility. The SFC request is a condition on specified assurance actions, not a general exception to operational duties. Whether footnote 5 preserves that condition for demonstration is contested.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"modality","explanation":"The provider provisions use should; register maintenance and requested location disclosure use must; valuation selection and monitoring use shall while supervision and review use should. These differences are preserved without deciding enforceability. An assurance statement, substantive performance, third-party verification and legal opinion are distinct actions.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"time","explanation":"No commencement date, response deadline or confirmation frequency is supplied. Remain indicates continuing responsibility; requests activate specified actions. Valuation supervision is ongoing and review periodic. Intermediary satisfaction with controls precedes related activities.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"dependencies","explanation":"Footnote 1 depends on applicable Appendix Part A factors and references 6.8. Footnote 5 depends on the complete requirements in paragraphs 10–13, which are not all supplied; the paragraph numbered 13 in unit 10 belongs to the separate intermediary context and cannot fill that gap. Footnote 2 and Appendix Parts A and B are absent. These gaps prevent a complete fulfillment assessment.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"}],"questions":["Which applicable standard determines sufficient management, operational soundness, ownership record keeping and smart-contract integrity, and how does it distinguish reasonable supervisory conduct from required outcomes?","What actual governance, outsourcing, intervention, monitoring, incident and ownership-reconciliation evidence shows both retained responsibility and performance?","What do the complete Product Provider paragraphs 10–13, footnote 2 and applicable Appendix provisions require?","Does footnote 5 specify the content of paragraph 14's request-conditioned demonstration, or independently require demonstration without a request?","Does high confidence qualify only the absence of smart-contract vulnerabilities and security flaws, or the full minimum assurance proposition? Does SFC satisfaction govern demonstration alone or confirmation as well?","What requests, if any, has the SFC made for demonstration, third-party audit or verification, legal opinion or register-location information, and with what scope and timing?","Which actor roles overlap in the actual arrangement, and what governing version, effective date and confirmation or response timing applies?"],"readings":[{"assumptions":["The selected slice identifies unit 0 as paragraph 10 and units 3, 5 and 6 as paragraphs 14, 15 and 16.","Continuing responsibility can coexist with delegation of operational tasks."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"6.8 The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802"},{"quote":"6.8 The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders. The Commission must be advised on request of the address(es) where the register is kept.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"quote":"6.11C Where a third party is engaged in the valuation of a scheme, the management company shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence in the selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring of such third party in ensuring such entity possesses the appropriate level of knowledge, experience and resources that commensurate with the appropriate valuation policies and procedures for each scheme. The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"quote":"13. Where intermediaries issue (eg, fund managers of tokenised funds) or are substantially involved in the issuance of the Tokenised Securities which they intend to deal in or advise on, they remain responsible for the overall operation of the tokenisation arrangement notwithstanding any outsourcing to third-party vendors/service providers.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"}],"distinction":"This reading treats paragraph 10 primarily as allocating non-transferable accountability. A service failure need not prove that responsibility was transferred, although it may prove a separate performance failure. It does not treat contractual allocation alone as fulfillment of all operational requirements.","family":"scope","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_allocation","questions":["What authority and practical ability to direct, obtain information from and remediate the outsourced arrangement must accompany retained accountability?","Does the applicable standard make particular supervisory conduct sufficient for the paragraph 10 control despite an operational failure?"],"statement":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 can be read principally as retaining full and ultimate accountability with the Product Provider despite outsourcing. Governance and contractual allocation, backed by actual authority and conduct consistent with that allocation, could support a conclusion that responsibility was retained. They do not alone establish sound operations or proper ownership records. Section 6.8 permits specified actors to maintain the register and separately requires requested disclosure of its location; it does not displace the provider's responsibility. Section 6.11C expressly prescribes care in selection and monitoring for valuation outsourcing, but the packet does not make that standard the tokenisation fulfillment test.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: retained accountability as the selected control"},{"assumptions":["Paragraph 10 is read with the supplied ownership-record requirement and paragraph 14 assurance provisions.","Operational evidence is assessed against an applicable standard, rather than an invented guarantee against every failure."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"17. Where intermediaries deal in, advise on, or manage portfolios investing in Tokenised Securities, intermediaries are reminded to conduct due diligence on the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers involved in the tokenisation arrangement as well as the features and risks arising from the tokenisation arrangement. 18. Intermediaries should understand and be satisfied with the controls implemented by the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers to manage ownership and technology risks of the Tokenised Securities before they engage in related activities. Please refer to Parts A and B of the Appendix to this circular for a non-exhaustive list of factors for consideration.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"distinction":"This reading makes substantive performance part of fulfillment of the responsibility requirement itself. Under the allocation reading, retained responsibility can be established separately from failed performance; here, authority and diligent supervision alone cannot establish fulfillment where required outcomes are absent.","family":"modality","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_performance","questions":["How does the applicable standard treat an incident despite diligent supervision, including materiality and remediation?","What evidence establishes operational compatibility, accurate ownership records and the other referenced requirements?"],"statement":"Read with the express requirement to ensure proper ownership records and operational compatibility, paragraph 10 can instead be understood as requiring the Product Provider to discharge substantive responsibility for the arrangement. A supported fulfillment conclusion would require evidence of retained accountability, effective supervisory conduct and operational and record-keeping performance meeting the applicable standard. Formal authority or an assertion of responsibility is insufficient; supervision is evidence of performance, not an automatic substitute for it. The intermediary due-diligence and prior satisfaction provisions concern separate actors and controls and cannot themselves establish provider fulfillment. The excerpt does not resolve whether every failure defeats fulfillment or whether an applicable standard permits a qualified assessment.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: substantive discharge of responsibility"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5 elaborates paragraph 14's assurance content while preserving its express request condition for demonstration.","The formalization tests only the activation condition for paragraph 14 demonstration, not performance, confirmation, licence status or overall compliance."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"This reading gives operative effect to the request phrase in paragraph 14 despite its omission from footnote 5. No request leaves confirmation and underlying duties in place but does not activate demonstration under the selected control.","family":"exceptions","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor is a Product Provider within the supplied paragraph 14 provision.","name":"is_product_provider","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to this provider for the demonstration described in paragraph 14; a request solely for another action does not automatically count.","name":"sfc_demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"sfc_demonstration_requested","result_type":"bool","scope":"is_product_provider"},"local_id":"demonstration_request_conditioned","questions":["Does the SFC-satisfaction standard attach only to demonstration, as its placement suggests?","Does high confidence attach only to the smart-contract proposition, as its placement suggests, or also to compliance with paragraphs 10–13?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] Within the Product Provider scope, true means paragraph 14's request condition for demonstration is satisfied; false means that condition is not satisfied. Confirmation remains unconditioned by a request. Both actions concern management and operational soundness, ownership record keeping and smart-contract integrity; footnote 5 sets a minimum content of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and absence of contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with high confidence. The strongest grammatical reading attaches SFC satisfaction to demonstration and high confidence to the smart-contract proposition, although broader attachment remains unresolved. With no request, demonstration is not activated on this reading; substantive duties and confirmation remain. Paragraph 15 separately conditions obtaining third-party audit or verification on a request and covers the same three subjects. Paragraph 16 separately conditions obtaining a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application on a request. Neither provision establishes a routine audit or opinion requirement in the absence of its request.","subject":"Paragraph 14 demonstration trigger with footnote 5 supplying minimum content"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5's unqualified direction to confirm and demonstrate has independent operative effect for its stated minimum content.","This reading retains paragraph 14's request condition for any broader demonstration and recognizes the resulting textual tension."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"Unlike the request-conditioned reading, the omission of a request qualification in footnote 5 creates an independently expected minimum demonstration. This is textually possible but weaker because it reduces the work done by paragraph 14's express condition. It supplies no basis for removing the separate request conditions in paragraphs 15 and 16.","family":"dependencies","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor is a Product Provider within the supplied paragraph 14 provision.","name":"is_product_provider","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to this provider for the demonstration described in paragraph 14; a request solely for another action does not automatically count.","name":"sfc_demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"true","result_type":"bool","scope":"is_product_provider"},"local_id":"demonstration_minimum_independent","questions":["Is footnote 5 independently operative or solely explanatory of the main paragraph?","If independently operative, when and in what form must the minimum demonstration be supplied without a request?","Does SFC satisfaction extend to the independently required minimum demonstration?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] Within the Product Provider scope, true means the condition for the minimum demonstration specified by footnote 5 is satisfied independently of an SFC request. The constant true expresses this proposed unconditional activation, not an invented fact or a finding that demonstration occurred; false does not arise within scope on this reading. Footnote 5 can be read as independently requiring both confirmation and demonstration to the SFC of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and the high-confidence smart-contract proposition. With no request, that minimum demonstration would remain expected, while paragraph 14 still expressly conditions its broader demonstration on a request. The precise confidence and satisfaction standards remain unresolved. Paragraphs 15 and 16 still require their respective requests for third-party audit or verification and a satisfactory supporting legal opinion; footnote 5 does not expressly expand those provisions.","subject":"Footnote 5 as an independent minimum demonstration requirement"}]}},"java_checks":[],"live_provider":true,"model_calls":2,"no_progress":0,"nodes":[],"pending":"action.9493c2a73fc746b880d5df3730458ed8","provider_id":"legal-gap.optional-tools.v1.compact.v2","retrievals":[],"rounds":0,"roundtrips":[],"run_id":"run.8adc12b432874c5bb1e8b4730a0ab93f","stop":null}